(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-gi-i-ii-quiz-version-4"]={"title":"Gastrointestinal Tract I & II — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 4)","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"Digestive organs are divided into two categories: the GI (alimentary) tract organs and the:","choices":["The lymphatic organs of immunity","Retroperitoneal organs","The endocrine organs of the body","Accessory digestive organs"],"answer":3,"exp":"The digestive system is divided into the GI (alimentary) tract and the accessory digestive organs, such as the liver, gallbladder, and pancreas, which assist digestion without forming the tube itself. — Slide 7 (General Structure)"},{"q":"Motility that pushes material along the GI tract is generated by which tissue in the wall?","choices":["Skeletal muscle throughout","Elastic cartilage","Cardiac muscle","Smooth muscle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Smooth muscle in the muscularis externa generates the peristaltic contractions that propel luminal contents along the GI tract. — Slide 8 (General Structure)"},{"q":"The lesser omentum extends from the porta hepatis to the lesser curvature of the stomach and comprises the hepatogastric and which other ligament?","choices":["Hepatoduodenal ligament","Falciform ligament","Coronary ligament","Splenorenal ligament"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lesser omentum is composed of the hepatogastric and hepatoduodenal ligaments, running from the porta hepatis to the lesser curvature and the first part (bulb) of the duodenum. — Slide 21 (Peritoneal Reflections)"},{"q":"The root of the mesentery proper runs from the duodenojejunal flexure to which location?","choices":["The pelvic brim","The left colic flexure","The right iliac fossa","The porta hepatis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The root of the mesentery proper runs obliquely from the duodenojejunal flexure down to the right iliac fossa, near the ileocecal junction. — Slide 22 (Mesentery)"},{"q":"The lips (labia) are covered externally by which epithelium?","choices":["Simple columnar absorptive epithelium","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Transitional (urothelial) epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium of serosal surfaces"],"answer":1,"exp":"The lips are covered externally by keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, which withstands the friction of the lip surface. — Slide 26 (Oral Cavity)"},{"q":"The mucosa of the esophagus is lined by which epithelium?","choices":["Simple cuboidal epithelium of small ducts","Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","Transitional urothelial-type epithelium"],"answer":1,"exp":"The esophageal mucosa is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, which tolerates the abrasion of a swallowed bolus without the drying needed for keratinization. — Slide 42 (Esophagus histology)"},{"q":"The lesser omentum attaches to the stomach along which curvature?","choices":["The anterior surface","The greater curvature","The lesser curvature","The pyloric antrum only"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lesser omentum attaches along the lesser curvature of the stomach, extending up to the porta hepatis of the liver. — Slides 21, 54 (Lesser Omentum / Stomach)"},{"q":"The pyloric region of the stomach includes the antrum, the canal, and the:","choices":["Cardia","Fundus","Sphincter","Rugae"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pyloric region of the stomach comprises the antrum, the pyloric canal, and the pyloric sphincter, which regulates the release of chyme into the duodenum. — Slide 54 (The Stomach)"},{"q":"The splenic artery, which supplies the spleen, is a branch of which larger vessel?","choices":["Celiac trunk","Superior mesenteric artery","Inferior mesenteric artery","Renal artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The splenic artery is one of the three terminal branches of the celiac trunk (with the left gastric and common hepatic arteries) and also supplies pancreatic branches en route to the spleen. — Slides 137, 139 (Splenic Artery / Celiac Trunk)"},{"q":"The white pulp of the spleen is functionally classified as which kind of tissue?","choices":["Hematopoietic filtering","Endocrine","Lymphatic","Adipose"],"answer":2,"exp":"The white pulp of the spleen is lymphatic tissue organized around central arterioles, responsible for the immune (lymphocyte) functions of the organ. — Slide 136 (The Spleen)"},{"q":"The first segment of the small intestine, which begins at the pyloric sphincter and is largely retroperitoneal, is the:","choices":["Duodenum","Jejunum","Ileum","Cecum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The duodenum is the first, shortest (~25 cm), and widest segment of the small intestine, beginning at the pyloric sphincter and lying largely retroperitoneal. — Slides 61, 66 (Duodenum)"},{"q":"The ileum terminates at which structure, which controls entry into the large intestine?","choices":["The pyloric sphincter","The major duodenal papilla","The duodenojejunal flexure","The ileocecal valve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ileum, the final segment of the small intestine, terminates at the ileocecal valve, which controls the passage of material into the cecum. — Slide 61 (Small Intestine)"},{"q":"Which segments of the colon are retroperitoneal?","choices":["The sigmoid colon and the rectum","Transverse and sigmoid colon","Cecum and the vermiform appendix","Ascending and descending colon"],"answer":3,"exp":"The ascending and descending colon are secondarily retroperitoneal, fixed to the posterior abdominal wall, while the transverse and sigmoid colon remain intraperitoneal and mobile. — Slides 93, 96 (Ascending / Descending Colon)"},{"q":"The blind pouch located posterior to the ileocecal valve that helps prevent reflux into the ileum is the:","choices":["Cecum","Rectum","Sigmoid colon","Appendix"],"answer":0,"exp":"The cecum is an intraperitoneal blind pouch lying posterior and inferior to the ileocecal valve, with the appendix hanging from its posteromedial wall. — Slide 85 (Cecum and Appendix)"},{"q":"Which region of the pancreas is a hook-shaped extension projecting from the head?","choices":["Pancreatic body","Pancreatic tail","Pancreatic neck","Uncinate process"],"answer":3,"exp":"The uncinate process is a hook-shaped extension of the pancreatic head that curls posterior to the superior mesenteric vessels. — Slide 130 (The Pancreas)"},{"q":"The pancreas has how many ducts conveying its exocrine secretions?","choices":["A main and an accessory duct","Only a single main duct","Three ducts","None — secretions enter directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pancreas drains its exocrine secretions through a main pancreatic duct (of Wirsung) and an accessory duct (of Santorini). — Slide 130 (Pancreatic ducts)"},{"q":"Which lobe of the liver is the largest?","choices":["Left lobe","Right lobe","Quadrate lobe","Caudate lobe"],"answer":1,"exp":"The right lobe is the largest lobe of the liver, separated from the smaller left lobe by the falciform ligament anteriorly and the fissure for the ligamentum venosum posteriorly. — Slide 115 (Lobes of the Liver)"},{"q":"The peritoneal ligaments that reflect onto the liver from the diaphragm and surround the bare area include the falciform, coronary, triangular, and:","choices":["Ligamentum teres","Splenorenal ligament","Phrenicocolic ligament","Broad ligament"],"answer":0,"exp":"The liver's peritoneal reflections include the falciform ligament (containing the ligamentum teres, the remnant of the umbilical vein), the coronary ligament, and the triangular ligaments, which together outline the bare area. — Slide 113 (Anatomy of the Liver)"},{"q":"After passing through hepatic sinusoids, blood collects into the central vein of each lobule and ultimately drains into the:","choices":["Portal vein → hepatic artery","Hepatic veins → inferior vena cava","Common bile duct → duodenum","Splenic vein → celiac trunk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Blood exits the hepatic sinusoids into the central vein of each lobule, collects into the hepatic veins, and drains directly into the inferior vena cava. — Slide 121 (Histology of the liver)"},{"q":"The hepatic artery supplies approximately what percentage of blood flow to liver tissue?","choices":["About 95%","About 50%","About 75%","About 15%"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hepatic artery supplies only about 15% of the blood flow to liver tissue (chiefly the bile ducts), while the portal vein delivers the remaining ~85% that needs metabolic processing. — Slide 108 (Liver triple blood supply)"},{"q":"Bile ultimately reaches the duodenum through an opening on the descending part of the duodenum called the:","choices":["Duodenojejunal flexure","Pyloric sphincter","Major duodenal papilla","Ileocecal valve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bile and pancreatic secretions enter the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla, an opening on the descending (second) part of the duodenum. — Slides 61, 66 (Duodenum)"},{"q":"The gallbladder's primary role in the biliary system is to:","choices":["Produce and secrete bile acids","Store and concentrate bile","Produce digestive enzymes","Filter blood-borne toxins"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gallbladder's primary role is to store and concentrate the bile that is produced by the liver, releasing it into the duodenum when stimulated. — Slides 104, 126 (Liver / Gall Bladder)"},{"q":"The superior mesenteric artery and inferior mesenteric artery are unpaired branches of which vessel?","choices":["The common hepatic artery","The celiac trunk","The abdominal aorta","The splenic artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries are unpaired midline branches of the abdominal aorta, supplying the midgut and hindgut respectively. — Slides 143–145 (SMA / IMA)"},{"q":"The left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature and also gives off which branches?","choices":["Renal branches","Esophageal branches","Gonadal branches","Phrenic branches only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach and also sends esophageal branches upward to the abdominal esophagus. — Slide 139 (Arterial Supply)"},{"q":"Unlike the portal system, the inferior vena cava carries blood directly to which chamber of the heart?","choices":["Right ventricle","Left atrium","Right atrium","Left ventricle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The inferior vena cava carries blood directly back to the right atrium, unlike portal venous blood, which must first pass through the liver before reaching systemic circulation. — Slides 108, 146 (hepatic veins / IVC)"},{"q":"The right gonadal and right suprarenal veins typically drain directly into the IVC, whereas on the left they usually drain into the:","choices":["Left renal vein","Hepatic portal vein","Azygos vein system","Splenic vein trunk"],"answer":0,"exp":"On the right side, the gonadal and suprarenal veins drain directly into the IVC, but on the left they usually drain first into the left renal vein because the IVC lies to the right of the aorta. — Slide 146 (Inferior Vena Cava)"},{"q":"The lymphatic drainage of the abdominal viscera ultimately converges toward which dilated lymphatic structure?","choices":["The splenic hilum","The thoracic inlet","The portal vein","The cisterna chyli"],"answer":3,"exp":"Lymph from the abdominal viscera converges toward the cisterna chyli, a dilated lymphatic sac that is the origin of the thoracic duct. — Slide 52 (lymphatic drainage)"},{"q":"Which arterial nodal groups organize the lymphatic drainage of the GI organs?","choices":["The external and internal iliac nodal groups only","The renal and gonadal nodal groups exclusively","Celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric groups","The phrenic and lumbar nodal groups exclusively"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lymph nodes draining the GI organs are organized into celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric groups, each paralleling the artery that supplies its corresponding gut territory. — Slide 52 (lymphatic drainage)"},{"q":"Aggregated lymphoid nodules (Peyer's patches) are a distinguishing histological feature of which small-intestinal segment?","choices":["Duodenum","Ileum","Jejunum","Pylorus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Aggregated lymphoid nodules (Peyer's patches) are a distinguishing histological feature of the ileum, reflecting its role in immune surveillance of the distal small intestine. — Slide 77 (Ileum)"},{"q":"The appendix is fixed to the cecum by a small mesentery called the:","choices":["Mesentery proper","Transverse mesocolon","Sigmoid mesocolon","Mesoappendix"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vermiform appendix is fixed to the cecum by its own small mesentery, the mesoappendix, and is a lymphoid organ dominated by lymphoid nodules. — Slide 85 (Cecum and Appendix)"}]};
